<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tugu inscription</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">5th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tugu inscription is a 5th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> inscription of King Purnawarman of Tarumanagara, found at Tugu in North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jakarta</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. It records the digging of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gomti-river">Gomati</a> canal, more than 11 kilometres long, in 21 days, and a gift of 1,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> on its completion. The longest of the Tarumanagara inscriptions, it is evidence of early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> kingship and organised labour in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tugu-inscription.jpg" alt="Tugu inscription" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bkusmono at Indonesian Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prasasti_tugu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
